## Context for this particular MIT grant

The license above is the actual legal terms, and it is deliberately the
plain, unmodified MIT text - no added field-of-use or non-commercial clause,
because that would stop this from being open source in any meaningful sense.
The paragraphs below are context on *why* this project exists and *how* it
was built, not additional legal conditions. Nothing here narrows the grant
above.

**This was written with the help of AI.** Large parts of the code, the
comments explaining it, and this documentation were produced in collaboration
with an AI coding assistant, under a human's direction and review. That is
disclosed here plainly rather than left for you to guess. It does not change
what the MIT license permits you to do with the code - but it is a fact worth
knowing before you decide how much to trust any given line without reading it
yourself.

**Do your own due diligence before running any of this - AI-assisted or not.**
`install.sh` and `install-windows.ps1` can reformat a physical disk, and this
stack is explicitly designed to run uncensored/abliterated models with no
content filter. Read a script before running it as root or as Administrator.
This applies to code from any source, not specifically because AI was
involved in writing it - but it applies here too.

**This was not built in a day.** It is a personal side project, developed
over an extended period out of curiosity about what was actually possible to
build this way - not a company's roadmap item, not a product with a support
commitment behind it, and not something rushed out. Bugs were found in
exactly the way you would expect from that: by running it for real, on real
hardware, repeatedly, and fixing what broke each time. See
[`docs/PRODUCTION_READINESS.md`](docs/PRODUCTION_READINESS.md) for an honest,
itemised account of what has and has not actually been verified.

**This is for personal curiosity, not business use.** It was built to see
what was possible, for one person's own hardware and own use, not as
something intended to be deployed commercially or relied on in a business
context. The MIT license does not and cannot legally forbid that use - MIT
never does, for anyone - but that was never the intent behind sharing it, and
you should weigh that before building anything you depend on financially on
top of a personal side project with a single, unpaid maintainer.

None of the above is a substitute for reading the code, and none of it
transfers any risk away from you. See the warranty disclaimer above - it is
the actual operative term, and it means exactly what it says.
